        "parms": "# midiin, xmidiin and midiparse — raw MIDI and taking it apart\nmidiin sends every byte that arrives on a MIDI input port as an int, in arrival order and uninterpreted. xmidiin frames the same bytes into messages and sends each one as a list, such as 144 60 100: a note-on for middle C, velocity 100, on channel 1. midiparse takes bytes, one at a time or as a list, and splits them into notes, controllers, program changes, pressure and pitch bend, with the channel.\nThe first argument is the input port, counted from 0; the midiinfo tutorial lists them. A port that cannot be opened, or no keyboard at all, leaves the object silent, and the patch still loads. On Windows a port can be open in one place at a time, so untick it under Settings › MIDI for a patch to hear it.\nAt most eight MIDI input objects, in all open patches together, can listen to one port. A ninth stays silent, and nothing says so (yse-soundengine#1246).\nMax's xmidiin sends one byte at a time, like midiin. Here it sends a whole message per list, so you can see where each message ends.",
        "type": ".text"
      },
