              "Object": 4
            },
            "Count": 1
          }
        }
      },
      "object 20": {
        "ID": 20,
        "gui": {
          "x": "640.0",
          "y": "460.0",
          "w": "300"
        },
        "parms": "3. Click 60, then 0. A message may leave out its status byte when it repeats, which is called running status, so these two bytes are another note-on: 60 0, a release. Both kinds of release come out as velocity 0.",
        "type": ".text"
      },
      "object 21": {
        "ID": 21,
        "gui": {
          "x": "640.0",
          "y": "570.0",
          "w": "300"
        },
        "parms": "4. The outlets, left to right: note, poly pressure and controller as pairs, then program (1-128), channel pressure, pitch bend, channel, and everything else as a list of bytes, such as a SysEx dump or a clock tick. Pitch bend is only the coarse byte, 0-127 with 64 at rest, as in Max; xbendin reads all 14 bits.",
        "type": ".text"
